course: language
lesson: 11
title: Grammar puzzles, part one
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Grammar puzzles are like maths puzzles with words: each has an exact answer, and the trick is to look at the job each word does in this sentence, not at what it usually is.
explain: The classic trap: "The old man the boat." It looks broken until you see that "the old" means old people and "man" is the verb (to crew a boat): old people crew the boat.
explain: To find who a verb agrees with, find the real subject: in "Each of the boys has a bike", the subject is "each" (one at a time), so it is "has", even though "boys" stands right next to the verb.
explain: Some sentences have two meanings and grammar alone can't choose: "I saw a man on a hill with a telescope" (who has the telescope?); the fix is to rewrite it.
explain: A comma can rescue a dog: "My hobbies are cooking my dog and reading" sounds terrible, but "My hobbies are cooking, my dog, and reading" is a happy list.
fact: In "The dog chased the cat into the garden" there are three nouns: dog, cat and garden.
fact: In "The old man the boat", "man" is the verb meaning to crew; a sentence that tricks you into reading it wrongly at first is called a garden-path sentence.
fact: Many yes/no questions are made by moving the helping verb in front of the subject: "The cat is sleeping" becomes "Is the cat sleeping?"
fact: In "She quickly ate the green apple", green is the adjective (it describes the apple) and quickly is the adverb (it says how she ate).
fact: The plurals of child, foot, box, baby and leaf are children, feet, boxes, babies and leaves.
fact: "Each of the boys has a bike" is correct, because "each" is the subject and means one at a time.
fact: The pasts of bring, teach, swim, fly and eat are brought, taught, swam, flew and ate; all five are irregular.
fact: "I saw a man on a hill with a telescope" is ambiguous: the telescope could be yours or the man's.
fact: Commas in a list separate the items; a comma before the last "and" is called the Oxford comma, and British writers often leave it out.
fact: "I can sing and I will dance" has four verbs: can and will are helping (modal) verbs, sing and dance are main verbs.
fact: With "neither ... nor", the verb agrees with the closer subject: "Neither the cat nor the dogs are hungry."
exercise: How many nouns are in "The dog chased the cat into the garden"? | 3 | 2 | 4 | 5
exercise: What is "into" in that sentence? | A preposition | A noun | A verb | An adjective
exercise: Find the verb in "The old man the boat." | man | old | boat | the
exercise: What does "The old man the boat" mean? | Old people crew the boat | An old man owns a little boat | The boat is very old and rusty | A man is as old as the boat
exercise: Turn "The cat is sleeping" into a yes/no question by moving one word. | Is the cat sleeping? | The cat is it sleeping? | Does the cat is sleeping? | Sleeping, is the cat?
exercise: In "She quickly ate the green apple", which is the adverb? | quickly | green | ate | apple
exercise: What are the plurals of child, foot, box, baby, leaf? | children, feet, boxes, babies, leaves | childs, foots, boxs, babys, leafs | children, feets, boxes, babies, leafs | childrens, feet, boxen, babyes, leaves
exercise: Which is correct? | Each of the boys has a bike | Each of the boys have a bike | Each of the boys having a bike | Each of the boy have bikes
exercise: Put into the past: I bring, I teach, I swim, I fly, I eat. | brought, taught, swam, flew, ate | bringed, teached, swimmed, flied, eated | brang, taught, swum, flown, ate | brought, teached, swam, flew, eaten
exercise: "I saw a man on a hill with a telescope." Who has the telescope? | It could be me or the man, so it's ambiguous | Always the man, because he is the one standing on the hill | Always me | Nobody, telescopes can't be had
exercise: Add commas to "My hobbies are cooking my dog and reading" so the dog is safe. | cooking, my dog, and reading | cooking my dog, and reading | cooking my, dog and reading | cooking my dog and, reading
exercise: How many verbs are in "I can sing and I will dance"? | 4 | 2 | 3 | 5
exercise: "Neither the cat nor the dogs ___ hungry." Which word? | are | is | am | be
exercise: Why "are" in that sentence? | The verb agrees with the closer subject, "the dogs" | It always agrees with the first subject in the sentence | Neither always takes are | Cats are always plural
exercise: How many adjectives are in "The tired little girl ate a warm, sticky bun quickly"? | 4 | 3 | 5 | 2
exercise: "The horse raced past the barn fell" is a famous garden-path sentence. Which verb tells what the horse finally did? | fell | raced | past | barn
test: Your mate goes "easy, there's four nouns in 'the dog chased the cat into the garden', 'cos 'chased' is a thing." What's wrong? | Chased is a verb; there are 3 nouns | Nothing, there are 4 nouns | There are 5 nouns, 'into' is one too | There are 2 nouns, garden is a place
test: A Gen Alpha kid says "'The old man the boat' is giving broken English, it's mid." ("Mid" means not good.) Is it broken? | No, "man" is the verb: old people crew the boat | Yes, it has no verb at all | Yes, it should be "The old man rows the boat" | No, "old" is the verb
test: A teacher asks "Turn 'The dog has eaten' into a question." Which is right? | Has the dog eaten? | Does the dog has eaten? | The dog eaten has it? | Is the dog has eaten?
test: Your cousin says "the verb always agrees with the nearest noun, innit, so 'each of the boys have'." What's right? | Each of the boys has; the subject is each | He's right, "have" agrees with "boys", the nearest noun | Each of the boy have | Each of the boys haves
test: What would happen if we wrote lists with no commas at all? | Some lists would accidentally say odd things, like cooking your dog | Nothing, because commas never change what a sentence means | Every list would become a question | Lists would become longer
test: A wee lad from Edinburgh says "Ken how mony verbs in 'I can swim'? Jist the wan, aye?" ("Ken" means do you know, "wan" means one.) Is he right? | No, there are two: can and swim | Yes, only swim | Yes, only can | No, there are three: I, can, swim
test: In a cartoon, a detective says "I saw the thief with binoculars" and arrests the man holding binoculars. Why could he be wrong? | The detective might have used the binoculars himself | Thieves never carry binoculars | The sentence has no verb | Binoculars is a plural-only noun
test: A boomer gran says "Neither the twins nor Tom is coming, dear." Is that right? | Yes, the verb agrees with Tom, the closer subject | No, it must be "are" because the twins are two people | No, neither always takes "am" | Yes, but only because she's a gran
test: Puzzle: how many of these are verbs? "jump, happy, think, quickly, is" | 3 | 2 | 4 | 5
test: Puzzle: in a made-up language, "nuk" means fish (one), "nukki" means fish (many), "dar" means bird (one). What does "darri" mean? | birds | a big bird | a bird's nest | one fish and one bird
test: Your mate says "'The complex houses married and single soldiers' is nonsense." (Here a complex is a group of buildings.) What is the verb? | houses, meaning gives homes to | married | complex | soldiers
exam: Ascertain the number of nouns contained within the sentence "The dog chased the cat into the garden". | Three | Two | Four | Five
exam: Identify the word functioning as the verb in the sentence "The old man the boat". | man | old | the | boat
exam: Select the formulation consistent with the rules of agreement in standard English. | Each of the boys has a bicycle | Each of the boys have a bicycle | Each of the boys are having bicycles | Each one of the boy have a bicycle
exam: In respect of the sentence "Neither the cat nor the dogs ___ hungry", determine the verb form required by the rule of proximity. | are | is | was | be
